sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of the cancel, not a step of it. 2026-11-15: the task VMs and their release are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md); a cancel starts no run. 2026-11-16: the place release workflows are deleted; `operation-task-cancel` keeps its inputs and its end check, and calls no release.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-13: a task copy ends through its Instance destroy. Orchestrator 2026-11-14: work that only the copy holds, or a copy that cannot be read, refuses the cancel unless the caller discards it; the cancel is recorded under the group lock, in one transaction with the destroy's admission, after the destroy's reads. A commit a publish pushed counts as on origin. 2026-11-16 (group 5615): published means content on the default branch, not commit SHAs: without discard the cancel of a copy runs the copy's `base` phase, the one task:sync runs, and its content read decides; a squash-merged or hand-carried copy cancels without discard.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:cancel
  %% kind: tasks.cancel
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Copy, Node<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Check before any change<br/>cancellable
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group is cancellable; otherwise `tasks.not_cancellable`
  %%> - every refusal here and in the next step comes before the cancel stops running work, so a refused cancel changes nothing
  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval
  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token
  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token
  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace
  %%> - for a task copy, content on the default branch counts too: the copy's latest content read (`work`, of a `task:sync` or a cancel run) is of the copy's current head, holds the approved commit in its history and found every committed path on the default branch
  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - otherwise the cancel refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and admits nothing: run `task:publish` first
  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request
  alt the task owns a task copy, without discard
    Note over GW: Record, start the run<br/>group lock · token
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - no Operation holds the copy, such as its create or an earlier destroy; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
    %%> - after the group's running work was asked to stop, no Operation still holds the group; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
    %%> - the copy's container runs; a stopped or asleep copy cannot be read, so it refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_workspace_unknown` and its `reason`
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - records the `tasks.cancel` Operation under the group's lock key and starts `operation-task-cancel` with the copy's intent, as `task:sync` captures it
    %%> - mints the read token as `task:sync` does (`contents: read`, valid one hour) into a vault key of the run; the run gets only the key name
    %%> Result:
    %%> - HTTP 202 with the running Operation; the group runs on, and its lock keeps every turn, check and publish out until the run ends
    GW->>SW: Start the run<br/>operation-task-cancel · copy
    SW->>N: Base, then read the work<br/>task-copy-base · base
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the `base` phase of `task:sync`: brings the current default head into `refs/remotes/origin/<default>` of the copy through its Node mirror and one bundle; no credential enters the copy
    %%> - then, as `agent`, one content read: every path that HEAD changed since its merge base with the default head (adds, edits, deletes, modes; a rename is a delete and an add) must hold the same content on the default head, or in a commit that a `task:publish` of the group pushed
    %%> - empty commits and commits that only repeat published content add nothing, so a squash-merged or hand-carried branch reads `published`
    %%> - also counts uncommitted and untracked files and stashes: those are never published
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `work`: `state` `clean` or `dirty`, `head`, the `paths` count and up to five names that differ, `uncommitted`, `untracked`, `stashes`, and `empty` when HEAD changed nothing
    SW->>N: Discard the base scratch<br/>task-copy-base · base-cleanup
    SW-->>GW: Run ended<br/>terminal event · work
    alt work clean
      Note over GW: Admit the copy's destroy<br/>instance.destroy · force
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - the destroy's checks and the Schedule stop come first: its replication slot check over SSH, then the stop of the copy's Schedules
      %%> - admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the cancel succeeds; its result names the destroy Operation and holds the read as `workspace`
      %%> - when the destroy succeeds, a task that still runs and its open subtasks are `cancelled`, and the task lets go of the copy; a `failed` task stays `failed`
      %%> - when it fails, the task runs on and keeps the copy; the next cancel reads and admits the destroy again
    else dirty, or the run failed
      Note over GW: Fail the cancel<br/>copy kept
      %%> Result:
      %%> - `dirty`: the cancel fails with `tasks.copy_unpublished_work`; its details carry the head, the differing paths and the counts
      %%> - a failed run: `tasks.copy_workspace_unknown`, naming the failed step; the copy stays as it was, apart from its `origin/<default>` ref
      %%> - the group runs on with its copy; publish the work, or cancel again with discard
    end
  else the task owns a task copy, with discard
    Note over GW: Record, then admit destroy<br/>one transaction · group lock
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - no Operation holds the copy or, after the group's running work was asked to stop, the group; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads nothing and starts no run: the destroy's checks and the Schedule stop come first, outside any transaction
    %%> - then, in the destroy's one transaction, records the cancel under the group's lock key first and admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force
    %%> - an Operation that takes the group lock first makes the record refuse with HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`; the transaction rolls back, so a refused cancel never admits the destroy; the copy's Schedules stay stopped
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the cancel is recorded as succeeded at once (R1); its result names the destroy Operation; the destroy ends the task as above
  else no task copy and no workspace
    Note over GW: End the task at admission<br/>R1: no run
    %%> When:
    %%> - the task holds no workspace, such as a `todo` task or one whose task VM was removed; its open Operations are cancelled first
    %%> - a task that still holds a workspace that is no task copy (a task VM workspace) refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.workspace_ownership_refused`: nothing can release it any more
    %%> Result:
    %%> - in the admission transaction, the task and its open subtasks are `cancelled` and the Operation is recorded as succeeded; no Swamp run starts (R1)
    %%> - the HTTP 202 already carries the succeeded Operation, so `task:show` reads `cancelled` from then on
  end
